<p>John Morton, Lord Chancellor under Henry VII, used a universal argument to demand gifts for the royal treasury:</p>
<ul>
<li>If a man lived well, he was obviously rich.</li>
<li>If a man lived modestly, he obviously had savings.</li>
</ul>
<p>Either way, he could afford the tax. Francis Bacon wrote, &#8220;There is a tradition of a dilemma that Bishop Morton &#8230; used to raise up the benevolence to higher rates; and some called it his Fork.&#8221;</p>
